<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cirebon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cirebon is a port city on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, on the border between Javanese and Sundanese lands. It became the seat of a Muslim sultanate in the 15th and 16th centuries under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-gunung-jati">Sunan Gunung Jati</a>, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wali-songo">Wali Songo</a>, and was later divided between the courts of Kasepuhan and Kanoman. It came under Mataram and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, and its palaces and batik preserve a mixed Javanese, Sundanese, Chinese and Islamic culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cirebon.jpg" alt="Cirebon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cahyo Ramadhani · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanctuary_of_Ong_Tien.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…helped pay off Dutch public debt and finance railways and infrastructure at home, but it entrenched poverty in the Javanese peasant economy, contributed to famines such as those in <b>Cirebon</b> (1843–1844) and Demak and Grobogan (1849–1850), and provoked sustained criticism, most famously in Multatuli's novel Max Havelaar (1860).</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…1629 Agung sent large armies against Batavia, the base of the Dutch East India Company under Jan Pieterszoon Coen, but both sieges failed; in 1629 Dutch ships destroyed the rice stores gathered for the army at Tegal and <b>Cirebon</b>, and thousands of soldiers died of hunger and disease.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…1522 Sunda made a treaty with the Portuguese of Malacca, allowing them to build a fort at Sunda Kalapa in return for help, but before the Portuguese returned, forces from Demak and <b>Cirebon</b> under Fatahillah captured Banten and, in 1527, Sunda Kalapa itself, defeating a Portuguese landing party.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1527 forces from Demak and <b>Cirebon</b>, led by Sunan Gunung Jati and his son Maulana Hasanuddin, took the port of Banten from the Sunda Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/banten-sultanate">Banten Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cirebon</b> grew as a port on the border between Javanese and Sundanese lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/cirebon-sultanate">Cirebon Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Galuh later came under <b>Cirebon</b> and Mataram, and its name survives in Ciamis regional identity.</blockquote><a href="/state/galuh-kingdom">Galuh Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
